From 2a94d4a72071455d4a0ba48d47659197e8b88953 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <ci@rdna-boosts.invalid>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 07:01:42 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] rdna-boosts: block 16: the host-expert tier -- one
 pinned per-expert pool that mirrors the arena when the budget allows

Block 16 replaces the mode-keyed host-expert tiers with ONE mechanism: a pinned per-expert
region (the bounded host pool) whose contents are laid out as the arena's COMPLEMENT first and
then, while capacity remains, the arena-resident experts in the same heat order.

  --host-experts pinned     the whole set as pinned per-TENSOR masters (escape hatch / A-B)
  -lme auto                 the whole host expert set when it fits MOE_HOST_LIMIT_FRAC (80 %) of
                            MemAvailable -> the pool is a MIRROR of it; otherwise MOE_HOST_POOL_FRAC
                            of the bytes (the historical not-fitting band, unchanged)
  -lme N                    N GiB of pinned per-expert pool: the complement, plus mirrored
                            residents while capacity lasts, i.e. a full mirror when N covers the set
  --host-experts pool       deprecated shorthand for 'auto'

Three properties make it uniform and safe:
  * the pool is a pinned per-host-tensor region addressed by (expert -> slot) with slot_state as
    the presence bitmap, heat-ranked, and a native fill (a resident is a 1.3 us D2D);
  * a pool-resident expert is read IN PLACE by any device's kernel, not just one GPU's: the
    cold-read geometry predicate is shared by the host-side admission decision and the
    kernel-side description, and the tensor-split slice offset is folded into the cold base;
  * a budget that covers the complement stops the un-load/re-read cycle: the pool keeps an
    expert's copy instead of dropping it because VRAM also holds it, so nothing is ever
    re-staged from the page cache during a run (measured h=1.0000, zero evictions, no re-reads).
  A budget too small for the complement keeps the historical bounded layout, which is what keeps
  a set that does not fit RAM from being pinned (page cache / O_DIRECT instead of a hard pin).

Measured on Qwen3.6-35B-A3B Q4_K_M, -ncmoe 40, 2 devices (both PCIe x8), --seed 42 --temp 0,
--ignore-eos, 5246-token prose for prefill, byte-identical text in every arm:

  arm                              prefill   decode n=300   decode n=2000   pinned RAM
  block 16 (-lme auto, MIRROR)      1318     69.8           82.5            18662 MiB
  block 15 baseline (--host-experts pinned)  1105.4   71.9      82.9      18662 MiB
  -lme 16 (86 % of the set)           --      66.5             --              16329 MiB
  -lme 8  (43 % of the set)           --      54.1             --               8165 MiB

So the default gains ~19 % prefill for ~0.5-2 % decode at the same pinned RAM, 1 device decodes
52.2 (text 4ae33f7bd65c), and the bounded arm is unchanged by construction.

Diagnostics carried in from the campaign (and made loud, on stderr, once per process, because
ggml's WARN is filtered at llama-cli's default level): a pinned host allocation that falls back to
pageable memory, and a DEGENERATE (mirrored) tensor split.

Gate hooks for a change here: the MoE host-expert path needs the coherence gate (same-seed greedy
text at 1 and 2 devices), scripts/check-gpu-faults.sh, and the MTP four-axis protocol;
-prefill A/Bs of the fill path must set GGML_SCHED_DEVGATHER=0.
